SDE compositor: with software OpenGL, windows show what they draw after they were bound.

SDE 0.2.0 Alpha 3.1. GLX_EXT_texture_from_pixmap lets a driver copy a
pixmap into the texture when the pixmap is bound (glXBindTexImageEXT):
what is drawn into the pixmap later is only sure to reach the texture
after the pixmap is bound again. Mesa's software OpenGL (llvmpipe,
softpipe: Xvfb, virtual machines without 3D, a machine without its
graphics driver) copies. picom's old GLX backend bound a damaged window
again (glx-no-rebind-pixmap turned that off, "known to break things on
some drivers (LLVMpipe, xf86-video-intel, etc.)"); the backends of picom
12 bind a window's pixmap only when the window is mapped or resized. With
software OpenGL a window then showed what it had at that moment:

* a window resized while its program was still busy kept its old content
  at its old size, and the part it grew by stayed see-through (the
  wallpaper or the windows behind showed), until something reset the
  compositor;
* what a program drew a moment after its window was mapped did not show,
  the window stayed see-through; later changes (hover highlights, typing)
  did not show either.

With this patch the GLX backend reports BACKEND_QUIRK_PIXMAP_SNAPSHOT when
its renderer is not accelerated (GLX_MESA_query_renderer, or the names of
Mesa's software renderers), and with such a backend a window that was
damaged since its image was made is bound again (release_image, then
bind_pixmap of the same named pixmap) before the next frame. Accelerated
drivers, whose textures follow their pixmaps, are not affected, and
glx-no-rebind-pixmap = true turns the new binding off. At startup the log
says "Software OpenGL (<renderer>): a window is bound again after it was
drawn." (info level).

SDE's tests/test_compositor_redraw.py checks this on Xvfb with the GLX
backend, and with XRender as a control.

